// What the pool does not yet do right, because the compiler does not. No
// finalizing gap is left to pin: a value assigned over through '&mut pool[r]'
// is finalized as it is replaced (finalize.cone).
//
// THE HOLE, which compiles today, so no check here can pin it: a Ref is not
// paired with the pool it came from (the design's invariant lifetimes, key and
// lock, are not built). Used with another pool of the same type it is checked
// there like any Ref:
//
//   fn mixed(a &mut Pool[Pt], b &mut Pool[Pt]) i64 {
//     imm r = a.add(new Pt(1, 2));
//     b[r].x;                    // 'b' has no such value (a panic), or, where
//   }                            // b's slot is at the same generation, b's
//                                // own value: the wrong one, but a live
//                                // value of the right type, never stray memory
//
// No longer a hole: a change of a pool's shape while a borrow from it is still
// to be used. A borrow from 'pool[r]' or '&mut pool[r]', or an Option from
// 'get' or 'getMut', held in a local freezes a local pool: 'remove', an 'add'
// (which may move every value), and moving or dropping it are refused while
// the borrow is live. A pool reached through a '&mut Pool' parameter, a field
// of 'self' or a 'Rc[mut, T]' owner is not frozen against reads, but a
// 'remove' or 'add' through the same name, through a second '&mut Pool'
// parameter that may be the same pool at the call, or by a call whose body (or
// a callee or global it reaches) changes a pool, is refused while the borrow
// is still to be used (error 1339, the shape-changing rule's parts (a) and
// (b); test/cases/region/region_flow_pool_twomut.cone and
// region_flow_pool_freeze.cone pin them):
//
//   fn frozen(p &mut Pool[Pt], q &mut Pool[Pt]) i64 {
//     imm r = p.add(new Pt(1, 2));
//     imm x = &mut p[r];
//     q.remove(r);               // refused: q may be p, and x reads the value
//     x.x;                       // 'remove' would have finalized
//   }
//
// Also no longer a hole: a borrow from a pool, bare or inside the Option 'get'
// and 'getMut' return, carries the pool's loan wherever it goes. Returning the
// Option from the function holding the pool, storing it through a parameter,
// and matching the borrow out of it and returning that are refused, as
// returning or storing away a bare 'pool[r]' is; kept in an outer variable
// and used after the pool ends, any of them is refused too. The compiler's
// suite pins each route (test/cases/region/region_flow_pool_escape.cone).
//
//   fn escapes() Option[&Pt] {
//     mut p = Pool[Pt].empty();
//     imm r = p.add(new Pt(1, 2));
//     p.get(r);                  // refused: the pool dies here, and the
//   }                            // borrow in the Option would be returned
//
// Held in a variable of the function and used while the pool lives, any of
// these borrows is fine.

mod gaps;

import testing use *;
import pool use *;

fn main() i32 {
  done();
}
